 ```
 
+##### onRetry(context)
+
+Type: `Function`
+
+Callback invoked only when another attempt is going to happen, right before the wait. Unlike `onFailedAttempt`, it is not called for the final failure, which makes it suited to counting retries. It receives the same context as `onFailedAttempt`.
+
+The function is called _after_ `onFailedAttempt`, `shouldConsumeRetry`, and `shouldRetry`, so it is not called when any of them ends the retrying. It is also not called on `AbortError`, on an abort through `signal`, on `TypeError` (except network errors), or if `retries` or `maxRetryTime` are exhausted.
+
+The function can return a promise, which is awaited before the wait starts. If it throws, all retries will be aborted and the original promise will reject with the thrown error.
+
+```js
+import pRetry from 'p-retry';
+
+const run = async () => { … };
+
+const result = await pRetry(run, {
+	onRetry: ({error, attemptNumber}) => {
+		metrics.increment('retries');
+	}
+});
+```
+
 ##### shouldRetry(context)
 
 Type: `Function`
@@ -181,6 +203,15 @@ The exponential factor to use.
 
 A `factor` of `0` is treated as `1`, which keeps the delay constant. A `factor` below `1` shrinks the delay on every retry until it rounds to `0`, and from then on no timer is scheduled. See the warning on `minTimeout`.
 
+##### initialDelay
+
+Type: `number`\
+Default: `0`
+
+The number of milliseconds to wait before the first attempt.
+
+It is not a retry delay, so it is not affected by `factor`, `randomize`, or `maxTimeout`, and it does not count towards `maxRetryTime`. If `signal` aborts during the wait, the promise rejects with the abort reason and `input` is never called. A value of `0` schedules no timer.
+
 ##### minTimeout
 
 Type: `number`\
